14:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1c",null,{"guide":{"date":"April 7–25, 1864","place":"Red River and Cane River corridor from Loggy Bayou and Grand Ecore to Alexandria, Louisiana","result":"After Banks’s defeats at Mansfield and Pleasant Hill, Porter abandoned the advance toward Shreveport and brought his fleet back to Grand Ecore under repeated attack. The gunboat Eastport struck a mine and was later destroyed when it could not be freed. Banks left Grand Ecore on April 21. At Monett’s Ferry, Emory used an unguarded ford to send Birge’s brigade behind Hamilton Bee’s blocking force, which withdrew and opened the road to Alexandria. The last Union troops crossed on April 24 and the head of the column reached Alexandria on April 25, but low water trapped much of Porter’s fleet above the city","facts":[{"label":"Porter left Grand Ecore","value":"April 7, 1864"},{"label":"Blair’s Landing fight","value":"April 12, 1864"},{"label":"Tom Green killed","value":"April 12, 1864"},{"label":"Fleet reunited at Grand Ecore","value":"April 15, 1864"},{"label":"Eastport struck a mine","value":"April 16, 1864"},{"label":"Banks left Grand Ecore","value":"April 21, 1864"},{"label":"Monett’s Ferry action","value":"April 23, 1864"},{"label":"Lead troops reached Alexandria","value":"April 25, 1864"}],"maps":[{"title":"Grand Ecore, the Red River, and Alexandria","caption":"Porter moved upriver from Grand Ecore toward Loggy Bayou, reversed course after learning of Banks’s defeat, and then followed the Red River back toward Alexandria.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-93.35%2C31.05%2C-91.95%2C32.20&layer=mapnik&marker=31.7546%2C-93.0860"},{"title":"Monett’s Ferry flank","caption":"Hamilton Bee blocked the ferry from a wooded bluff. Emory demonstrated at the crossing while Henry Birge’s brigade used an unguarded ford about two miles away and approached the Confederate rear.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/louisiana.htm","image":"/images/monetts-ferry-tactical-map.svg","alt":"Schematic battle map of the Union flanking movement at Monett’s Ferry"},{"title":"The Red River campaign route","caption":"The period map shows the campaign corridor between Alexandria and Shreveport, including Mansfield and Pleasant Hill. The Red and Cane Rivers constrained both Union withdrawal routes.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2008626486/","image":"/images/red-river-campaign-1864-map.jpg","alt":"Historical map of the Red River campaign in 1864"}],"moments":[{"time":"1:18","audioTime":78,"title":"The narrative returned to Louisiana","detail":"Banks and Porter were both withdrawing after the April 8 defeat at Mansfield and the April 9 fighting at Pleasant Hill."},{"time":"2:04","audioTime":124,"title":"Porter had left Grand Ecore on April 7","detail":"He selected shallow-draft, heavily armed vessels for the final approach toward Shreveport because the Red River was falling, narrow, and sharply curved."},{"time":"4:11","audioTime":251,"title":"The fleet escorted army transports","detail":"About twenty transports carried supplies and roughly 1,600 XVII Corps soldiers under T. Kilby Smith. The transports were armed and protected with cotton bales and sacks of oats."},{"time":"4:52","audioTime":292,"title":"Low water delayed the movement","detail":"Iberville ran aground on April 8. Confederates had reduced the river’s flow by destroying the Tone’s Bayou dam on March 18."},{"time":"6:10","audioTime":370,"title":"Poor maps left Porter uncertain of his position","detail":"Federal charts misplaced and misnamed waterways near Loggy Bayou and Springfield Landing."},{"time":"7:19","audioTime":439,"title":"A scuttled steamer blocked the river","detail":"Confederates placed New Falls City across the channel, preventing the fleet from continuing toward Shreveport."},{"time":"8:14","audioTime":494,"title":"Kilby Smith secured the riverbank","detail":"The growing Confederate presence convinced Porter that Banks had probably been defeated."},{"time":"8:57","audioTime":537,"title":"The fleet learned of Banks’s retreat","detail":"William Andres and fifty cavalrymen reported the fighting at Mansfield and Pleasant Hill and ordered Kilby Smith back to Grand Ecore."},{"time":"9:39","audioTime":579,"title":"The vessels immediately turned back","detail":"Porter expected Confederate cavalry and artillery to attack the fleet along the confined river."},{"time":"10:43","audioTime":643,"title":"Large ships had to back downstream","detail":"The channel was too narrow for them to turn. Snags, grounding, damaged steering gear, and continuous musket fire slowed the withdrawal."},{"time":"11:57","audioTime":717,"title":"The fleet reached Blair’s Landing","detail":"On April 12, about 2,500 Texas cavalrymen under Tom Green attacked from concealed positions supported by four artillery pieces."},{"time":"12:55","audioTime":775,"title":"Osage grounded under fire","detail":"The transport Black Hawk, tied alongside the monitor, was struck repeatedly and most people aboard took shelter inside Osage."},{"time":"13:34","audioTime":814,"title":"Lexington silenced the Confederate battery","detail":"The timberclad used its eight-inch guns for about an hour while Osage worked free."},{"time":"14:28","audioTime":868,"title":"Tom Green was killed","detail":"After Osage broke free, one of its eleven-inch guns fired grapeshot into Green’s cavalry and killed the Confederate commander."},{"time":"15:15","audioTime":915,"title":"John Warner grounded","detail":"The supply ship could not be freed on April 13. Fort Hindman stayed behind until it pulled the vessel loose on April 15."},{"time":"16:00","audioTime":960,"title":"The fleet reunited with the army","detail":"The last vessels reached Grand Ecore on April 15."},{"time":"18:07","audioTime":1087,"title":"Banks still proposed another advance","detail":"He told Grant that the Confederates were on the defensive and said he intended to try another route to Shreveport."},{"time":"18:38","audioTime":1118,"title":"Banks retained A. J. Smith’s troops","detail":"Sherman demanded their return for the Atlanta campaign, but Banks used his seniority to keep them in Louisiana."},{"time":"19:23","audioTime":1163,"title":"Banks removed subordinates","detail":"He replaced chief of staff Charles Stone with William Dwight and relieved cavalry commanders Albert Lee and Napoleon Dudley."},{"time":"21:08","audioTime":1268,"title":"The army and fleet committed to Alexandria","detail":"Porter said falling water made a naval withdrawal unavoidable. Banks then said the army could not advance without naval support."},{"time":"21:38","audioTime":1298,"title":"Eastport struck a mine","detail":"The ironclad hit a Confederate mine on April 16, was refloated five days later, and grounded again near Montgomery."},{"time":"23:01","audioTime":1381,"title":"Porter destroyed Eastport","detail":"With water falling at Alexandria, he abandoned and blew up the immobilized ironclad on April 26 rather than delay the fleet further."},{"time":"23:20","audioTime":1400,"title":"Confederate artillery attacked the remaining ships","detail":"At the Cane River mouth a battery sank one support vessel, disabled another, and hit Cricket thirty-eight times, causing twenty-five casualties."},{"time":"24:23","audioTime":1463,"title":"Low water trapped the fleet at Alexandria","detail":"The river had fallen six feet, leaving most vessels unable to pass the rapids below town."},{"time":"25:05","audioTime":1505,"title":"Banks left Grand Ecore on April 21","detail":"His column marched south along Cane River while Porter’s vessels used the roughly parallel Red River channel."},{"time":"26:00","audioTime":1560,"title":"Taylor tried to close Monett’s Ferry","detail":"If Confederates held the southern end of the island between the rivers, the Union column could be trapped."},{"time":"27:10","audioTime":1630,"title":"The column marched twenty miles overnight","detail":"The lead stopped at 2:30 a.m. on April 22 so the rear guard could close up."},{"time":"28:13","audioTime":1693,"title":"Bee held the ferry crossing","detail":"About 1,600 Confederate cavalrymen and four artillery batteries occupied the wooded bluff across the river."},{"time":"28:51","audioTime":1731,"title":"An unguarded ford was found","detail":"A local enslaved man showed Union cavalry a crossing about two miles from the ferry."},{"time":"29:32","audioTime":1772,"title":"Birge approached the Confederate rear","detail":"His brigade crossed at the ford, made a wide flanking march, and reached the ridge without detection."},{"time":"30:24","audioTime":1824,"title":"Emory fixed Bee at the ferry","detail":"Union artillery and an infantry feint forced Bee to divide attention between his front and Birge’s force behind him."},{"time":"31:04","audioTime":1864,"title":"Bee withdrew","detail":"An incorrect report of another threat to his flank contributed to the decision. The withdrawal opened the crossing before Taylor could complete the trap."},{"time":"31:37","audioTime":1897,"title":"Banks’s army escaped the island","detail":"The final troops crossed on April 24, and the lead elements reached Alexandria the next afternoon."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Nathaniel P. Banks","command":"Department of the Gulf","subordinates":"Directed the army’s withdrawal from Grand Ecore"},{"commander":"William H. Emory","command":"XIX Corps","subordinates":"Secured Monett’s Ferry and coordinated the feint and flank"},{"commander":"Henry W. Birge","command":"XIX Corps brigade","subordinates":"Crossed the unguarded ford and attacked Bee’s rear"},{"commander":"A. J. Smith","command":"XVI and XVII Corps detachments","subordinates":"Retained by Banks instead of returning to Sherman"},{"commander":"T. Kilby Smith","command":"XVII Corps river detachment","subordinates":"Commanded about 1,600 soldiers aboard the transports"},{"commander":"David Dixon Porter","command":"Mississippi Squadron","subordinates":"Led the naval advance and withdrawal on the Red River"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"Richard Taylor","command":"District of West Louisiana","subordinates":"Tried to trap Banks north of Monett’s Ferry"},{"commander":"Tom Green","command":"Texas cavalry division","subordinates":"Attacked Porter’s fleet at Blair’s Landing"},{"commander":"Hamilton P. Bee","command":"Cavalry blocking force at Monett’s Ferry","subordinates":"Held 1,600 cavalrymen and four artillery batteries on the south bank"}]}],"people":[{"name":"William H. Emory","role":"Directed the Union action that opened Monett’s Ferry","side":"United States"},{"name":"Henry W. Birge","role":"Led the flanking brigade across the unguarded ford","side":"United States"},{"name":"T. Kilby Smith","role":"Commanded the troops protecting the army transports","side":"United States"},{"name":"Nathaniel P. Banks","role":"Led the army’s withdrawal from Grand Ecore to Alexandria","side":"United States","image":"/images/nathaniel-p-banks.jpg","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nathaniel_P._Banks_portrait.jpg"},{"name":"David Dixon Porter","role":"Brought the gunboats and transports back down the Red River","side":"United States","image":"/images/david-dixon-porter.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/exhibits/civil-war-in-america/biographies/david-dixon-porter.html"},{"name":"Richard Taylor","role":"Directed the Confederate effort to cut off Banks at Monett’s Ferry","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/richard-taylor.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666550/"},{"name":"Hamilton P. Bee","role":"Commanded the Confederate blocking position at Monett’s Ferry","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Tom Green","role":"Commanded the Texas cavalry attack at Blair’s Landing","side":"Confederacy"}],"losses":[{"name":"Tom Green","side":"Confederacy","role":"Major general commanding Texas cavalry","outcome":"Killed by grapeshot from USS Osage at Blair’s Landing on April 12, 1864."}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/blairs-landing-battlefield.jpg","alt":"Present-day view of the Blair’s Landing battlefield beside the Red River","caption":"At Blair’s Landing, Confederate cavalry and artillery fired into Porter’s downstream column from the riverbank.","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blair%27s_Landing_Battlefield_Louisiana.jpg"},{"image":"/images/monetts-ferry-battlefield.jpg","alt":"Present-day view of the Monett’s Ferry battlefield","caption":"The Monett’s Ferry position controlled the Union army’s principal route off the island between the Cane and Red Rivers.","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monett%27s_Ferry_Battlefield_Louisiana.jpg"},{"image":"/images/david-dixon-porter.jpg","alt":"Portrait of David Dixon Porter","caption":"Porter’s squadron had to reverse down narrow reaches of the Red River while under attack.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/exhibits/civil-war-in-america/biographies/david-dixon-porter.html"},{"image":"/images/nathaniel-p-banks.jpg","alt":"Portrait of Nathaniel P. Banks","caption":"Banks withdrew from Grand Ecore after the defeats at Mansfield and Pleasant Hill.","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nathaniel_P._Banks_portrait.jpg"},{"image":"/images/richard-taylor.jpg","alt":"Portrait of Richard Taylor","caption":"Taylor’s attempt to close Monett’s Ferry failed when Bee withdrew from the crossing.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666550/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Civil War Podcast episode 475","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2024/11/24/civil-war-podcast-episode-475/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Red River campaign map","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2008626486/"},{"label":"National Park Service: Louisiana Civil War operations","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/louisiana.htm"},{"label":"Library of Congress: David Dixon Porter","url":"https://www.loc.gov/exhibits/civil-war-in-america/biographies/david-dixon-porter.html"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Richard Taylor portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666550/"}]}}]}]
